WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.402
-Our next guest is
an Emmy-nominated actor,

00:00:02.402 --> 00:00:04.037
you know from shows
like "Deadwood"

00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:06.673
and the John Wick films.
He stars in "American Star,"

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:09.375
which is in theaters
January 26th. Let's take a look.

00:00:09.375 --> 00:00:13.980
-You and I both know that you
haven't the slightest intention

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:18.217
of buying a house on the island.
Right?

00:00:18.217 --> 00:00:21.254
-♪ I dream of heaven
and living hell ♪

00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:23.790
♪ Till I can't take it anymore ♪

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:27.627 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:29.061
♪ I'm too far gone
to turn around ♪

00:00:29.061 --> 00:00:32.699
♪ Don't hurt me again, okay? ♪

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:33.700
-Please welcome back
to the show

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.735
the one, the only
Ian McShane.

00:00:35.735 --> 00:00:37.737
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:37.737 --> 00:00:52.018 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:52.018 --> 00:00:53.186
-Hello!

00:00:53.186 --> 00:00:55.921
-Welcome back, Ian!
I'm so happy to have you.

00:00:55.921 --> 00:00:58.291
-Thank you for asking me back.
It's nice to be here.

00:00:58.291 --> 00:00:59.826
How are you?

00:00:59.826 --> 00:01:02.462
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:02.462 --> 00:01:04.096
-I, uh --
we were talking backstage.

00:01:04.096 --> 00:01:05.297
I was at the Emmys on Monday.

00:01:05.297 --> 00:01:06.665
I had a wonderful time.
-You lost.

00:01:06.665 --> 00:01:08.935
-I did, yeah, I mean --
-I'm not speaking to you.

00:01:08.935 --> 00:01:10.103
-I was trying
to keep it from them.

00:01:10.103 --> 00:01:12.038
They're gonna be heartbroken.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:12.038 --> 00:01:15.842
Uh, you -- you had described
award shows, and it's a very --

00:01:15.842 --> 00:01:17.043
You don't pull
your punches, Ian.

00:01:17.043 --> 00:01:18.944
I've never known you to be...
-That's right.

00:01:18.944 --> 00:01:20.213
-You've described them
as the most

00:01:20.213 --> 00:01:21.580
boring [bleep] things
in the world.

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:23.416
-Oh, Jesus Christ.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:25.918
Well, for a start, when
you're on, if you're nominated,

00:01:25.918 --> 00:01:27.654
they put you
on the end of a row.

00:01:27.654 --> 00:01:28.755
-Yeah.
-So there's a camera

00:01:28.755 --> 00:01:30.089
permanently, then you lose.

00:01:30.089 --> 00:01:32.224
You want to say, "Get me
the [bleep] out of here."

00:01:32.224 --> 00:01:34.827
[ Laughter ]
I mean, really.

00:01:34.827 --> 00:01:36.495
I mean,
it's so political now.

00:01:36.495 --> 00:01:39.465
Who knows? Next year,
they'll be showing season

00:01:39.465 --> 00:01:41.834
three of "The Bear,"
season one of "Succession,"

00:01:41.834 --> 00:01:43.870
and they won't know which year
they're going --

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:45.237
You know, this year,
'cause they all --

00:01:45.237 --> 00:01:47.006
it's all screwed up
because of the pandemic.

00:01:47.006 --> 00:01:50.209
So there'd be season 1
of "Deadwood" is up --

00:01:50.209 --> 00:01:53.145
-Maybe you'll win,
finally, for "Deadwood."

00:01:53.145 --> 00:01:56.682
-That'll be in the mistake
if I did that, too. No.

00:01:56.682 --> 00:01:58.685
I got to go --
Golden Globes is the funniest.

00:01:58.685 --> 00:02:01.520
I won that with Mariska Hargitay
the same year.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:04.023
-Yeah. It's a good year.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:04.023 --> 00:02:06.292
-But that was the least --
you could sort of, you know,

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:08.128
mingle and people have a drink,

00:02:08.128 --> 00:02:09.662
they can have a cigarette.
But now...

00:02:09.662 --> 00:02:11.831
But at Emmys and all Oscars,
you're like this.

00:02:11.831 --> 00:02:13.499
-Yeah.
-And everybody's secretly

00:02:13.499 --> 00:02:15.301
is, like, saying,
"Oh, you won!

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:17.703
Congratulations.
[Bleep] [ Mumbling ]

00:02:17.703 --> 00:02:19.672
[ Laughter ]
Resentment abounds.

00:02:19.672 --> 00:02:21.974
-You also don't care
for the description of someone

00:02:21.974 --> 00:02:23.509
as a national treasure.

00:02:23.509 --> 00:02:25.377
-Oh, God, no.
It sounds like you're dead.

00:02:25.377 --> 00:02:26.479
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:26.479 --> 00:02:28.614
And should be a tea cozy
over your head,

00:02:28.614 --> 00:02:31.084
you know, with tea brewing,
whatever.

00:02:31.084 --> 00:02:32.385
Well, that's
an English phrase --

00:02:32.385 --> 00:02:34.053
"Oh, he's a national treasure,
isn't he?

00:02:34.053 --> 00:02:36.555
And he's so lovely.
He's been around so long.

00:02:36.555 --> 00:02:38.124
Why don't we bury him, you know?
Why not?"

00:02:38.124 --> 00:02:40.360
[ Laughter ]

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:43.796
-Now, this -- this film,
"American Star," you star in it,

00:02:43.796 --> 00:02:45.264
you produced it.
-Yep.

00:02:45.264 --> 00:02:46.665
-And you play a hit man.

00:02:46.665 --> 00:02:47.934
And, you know,
you also, obviously,

00:02:47.934 --> 00:02:49.368
are a big part of
the John Wick universe,

00:02:49.368 --> 00:02:51.070
but this is a very different
kind of hit man.

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:53.172
-I tell you, it goes back
to about six years ago.

00:02:53.172 --> 00:02:55.808
I made a film with this
very gifted filmmaker,

00:02:55.808 --> 00:02:57.276
Gonzalo López-Gallego.

00:02:57.276 --> 00:02:59.545
We made a movie
called "Hollow Point,"

00:02:59.545 --> 00:03:01.513
with Patrick Wilson
and John Leguizamo,

00:03:01.513 --> 00:03:03.683
which is a sort of a --
a Western noir.

00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:04.950
And it was really good.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:07.119
But like a lot of indie --
independent movies,

00:03:07.119 --> 00:03:08.754
got lost in the shuffle
years ago

00:03:08.754 --> 00:03:11.290
with whatever -- distribution.
But this was --

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:14.127
He wanted to make a film
with me again, and so did I.

00:03:14.127 --> 00:03:17.696
And so he created this, and
with the writer Nacho Faerna.

00:03:17.696 --> 00:03:20.466
It took us four years,
and then we found a producer,

00:03:20.466 --> 00:03:22.769
the Spanish producers,
and my friend Michael Elliott,

00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:24.837
who I made a couple
of other movies with,

00:03:24.837 --> 00:03:29.976
and we had a window of time in,
um, what, spring '22 to make it.

00:03:29.976 --> 00:03:31.510
And we did,
and it was like five weeks

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:35.114
on this amazing island,
Fuerteventura.

00:03:35.114 --> 00:03:37.417
And it's the story of a guy --
I mean, he's a hit man,

00:03:37.417 --> 00:03:40.086
but he works for a
government agency or whatever.

00:03:40.086 --> 00:03:42.355
He commits
his own cardinal sin.

00:03:42.355 --> 00:03:44.224
He goes to an island,
does the job --

00:03:44.224 --> 00:03:45.791
You know, you go in,
you do the job,

00:03:45.791 --> 00:03:47.493
you get out -- always.

00:03:47.493 --> 00:03:49.829
But, no, there's a little
intelligence failure,

00:03:49.829 --> 00:03:51.197
and he's forced to stay.

00:03:51.197 --> 00:03:53.099
And instead of getting out,
he stays.

00:03:53.099 --> 00:03:55.568
And that's when everything
gets muddled up.

00:03:55.568 --> 00:03:56.935
And that -- the clip you showed,

00:03:56.935 --> 00:04:00.206
that's the great French
actress Fanny Ardant,

00:04:00.206 --> 00:04:02.041
who was Madame Truffaut,

00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:04.377
the widow of Monsieur Truffaut,
and was wonderful.

00:04:04.377 --> 00:04:07.547
She came on it
for a couple of days, and, uh --

00:04:07.547 --> 00:04:11.784
It's a -- It's a really tight
European noir movie with --

00:04:11.784 --> 00:04:14.186
It's unexpected.
It's not --

00:04:14.186 --> 00:04:16.389
I said I wanted to,
you know, after John Wick,

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:18.558
"Can I shoot somebody,
please, for a change?"

00:04:18.558 --> 00:04:19.826
[ Laughter ]

00:04:19.826 --> 00:04:21.827
Well, I do, but this isn't
a shoot-'em, bang-'em-up.

00:04:21.827 --> 00:04:24.196
But there's moments of that
in here. But it's more...

00:04:24.196 --> 00:04:25.564
-You shot in the Canary Islands.
-Yeah.

00:04:25.564 --> 00:04:28.133
-Beautiful beach.
Do you enjoy the beach?

00:04:28.133 --> 00:04:29.869
-I live in the beach.
I live on Venice Beach.

00:04:29.869 --> 00:04:31.637
-Oh, interesting.
'Cause I don't like the beach.

00:04:31.637 --> 00:04:33.172
-No?
-No.

00:04:33.172 --> 00:04:34.673
-Well, you'd like Venice Beach
because it's not really a beach.

00:04:34.673 --> 00:04:37.176
You can't see it half the time.
-So you're --

00:04:37.176 --> 00:04:39.212
-It's covered in marine layer.
No, I like --

00:04:39.212 --> 00:04:41.013
-Your favorite beach
is the one you can't see.

00:04:41.013 --> 00:04:42.314
[ Laughter ]

00:04:42.314 --> 00:04:43.682
-No, you can.
I'm saying --

00:04:43.682 --> 00:04:45.083
-But you've lived
on the beach for a long time.

00:04:45.083 --> 00:04:46.152
-Yeah, I've lived there
20 years.

00:04:46.152 --> 00:04:47.586
But, also, I like the city.

00:04:47.586 --> 00:04:49.689
So I go from the middle
of London, Marylebone,

00:04:49.689 --> 00:04:51.156
to living in Venice.
-Okay.

00:04:51.156 --> 00:04:52.525
-And we both --
My wife loves it there.

00:04:52.525 --> 00:04:53.993
And she's American.
I mean, we love --

00:04:53.993 --> 00:04:56.662
You know, we also live
in her hometown sometimes.

00:04:56.662 --> 00:04:58.264
She's got
a place in Michigan.

00:04:58.264 --> 00:05:00.632
-All right.
-And we vary. It's lovely.

00:05:00.632 --> 00:05:03.335
-When you're
an Englishman on the beach,

00:05:03.335 --> 00:05:04.870
do you feel like
you've escaped your fate,

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:05.971
that you, like, actually
got out?

00:05:05.971 --> 00:05:07.372
-No, 'cause I don't wear
Speedos, you know.

00:05:07.372 --> 00:05:09.475
-You don't.
-No, I don't go --

00:05:09.475 --> 00:05:11.744
Budgie -- Budgie smugglers,
as they're called now.

00:05:11.744 --> 00:05:13.379
[ Laughter ]

00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:14.813
-You know what, though?
I'll tell you this.

00:05:14.813 --> 00:05:17.016
You got the body for it, Ian.
You got the body for it.

00:05:17.016 --> 00:05:18.450
You've had the body for it
for a long time.

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:20.386
-Oh, my goodness.
-We went and did some research.

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:24.056
In your early work, you were
often in bed, shirtless.

00:05:24.056 --> 00:05:25.991
This is, uh...
[ Cheering ]

00:05:25.991 --> 00:05:27.292
There we go.
There's another.

00:05:27.292 --> 00:05:28.895
Look at Ian, you guys.

00:05:28.895 --> 00:05:30.096
-Ooh!

00:05:30.096 --> 00:05:31.264
-Um...
-Shocking.

00:05:31.264 --> 00:05:32.765
-Put my glasses on,
I'll have to have a look.

00:05:32.765 --> 00:05:34.366
-"Pussycat, Pussycat,
I Love You,"

00:05:34.366 --> 00:05:36.435
which is weirdly
not an adult film.

00:05:36.435 --> 00:05:38.170
[ Laughter ]

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:41.273
-It was the sequel
to "Pussycat, Pussycat."

00:05:41.273 --> 00:05:42.708
And it was, uh --

00:05:42.708 --> 00:05:45.912
Um, I was a writer
with seven mistresses.

00:05:45.912 --> 00:05:48.180
And, uh, what was it?
Who was it? What was it?

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:50.649
Um, it's the most
bizarre film ever made.

00:05:50.649 --> 00:05:53.152
I mean, the great thing was,
it was in Rome for eight weeks.

00:05:53.152 --> 00:05:55.288
And sometimes you say, "What do
you get out of the film?"

00:05:55.288 --> 00:05:57.623
You say, "I got
a good feeling from Rome."

00:05:57.623 --> 00:05:58.891
[ Laughter ]

00:05:58.891 --> 00:06:01.393
-Is that your way of saying
we shouldn't go rent it?

00:06:01.393 --> 00:06:03.729
-No, have a look if you want!
It's quite --

00:06:03.729 --> 00:06:05.931
It's a -- It's a --
it's a crazy, crazy movie.

00:06:05.931 --> 00:06:07.699
One of those crazy
late '60s movies.

00:06:07.699 --> 00:06:09.168
-Do you have to see
"Pussycat, Pussycat"

00:06:09.168 --> 00:06:10.636
to know what's going on?
[ Laughter ]

00:06:10.636 --> 00:06:12.371
-Uh...
-Do I have to see the first one?

00:06:12.371 --> 00:06:13.873
-"Pussycat, Pu--"
You just like saying "pussycat,"

00:06:13.873 --> 00:06:14.974
don't you?
-I do.

00:06:14.974 --> 00:06:16.909
[ Laughter ]
-I know you do.

00:06:16.909 --> 00:06:18.711
-I'm breaking through
a lot of walls today,

00:06:18.711 --> 00:06:20.179
thanks to you and Renée.
I really am.

00:06:20.179 --> 00:06:21.680
-Well, the first movie
was great --

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:24.116
"Pussycat, Pussycat."
Uh, what was it?

00:06:24.116 --> 00:06:25.384
That was
Peter Sellers playing --

00:06:25.384 --> 00:06:27.753
Remember that?
That bizarre comedy?

00:06:27.753 --> 00:06:29.255
-You did a --
What do you call these?

00:06:29.255 --> 00:06:31.157
Uh...
-Don't look at this.

00:06:31.157 --> 00:06:32.491
[ Laughter ]

00:06:32.491 --> 00:06:35.694
-This is "Cosmopolitan."
It's a centerfold.

00:06:35.694 --> 00:06:37.696
-That was England's first...
-The first male centerfold.

00:06:37.696 --> 00:06:39.364
-I was the first centerfold.
-All right.

00:06:39.364 --> 00:06:40.533
So, there we go.
-1970.

00:06:40.533 --> 00:06:43.503
-19-- Look at that.
[ Cheering ]

00:06:43.503 --> 00:06:46.071
Now, uh, two questions.
What was your --

00:06:46.071 --> 00:06:47.739
-That was my dog!
-That was your dog.

00:06:47.739 --> 00:06:49.341
-Moriarty.
-That --

00:06:49.341 --> 00:06:50.909
I'm so happy it was your dog.

00:06:50.909 --> 00:06:52.811
-Oh, yeah, it was Moriarty.
-Yeah.

00:06:52.811 --> 00:06:54.246
Well, it wouldn't be
somebody else's dog.

00:06:54.246 --> 00:06:56.882
Otherwise, I'd be up --
I'd be up for some kind of

00:06:56.882 --> 00:06:58.985
animal abuse or whatever.
-I would say if it was

00:06:58.985 --> 00:07:00.886
someone else's dog, they
wouldn't have taken it back.

00:07:00.886 --> 00:07:02.555
-Will you please stop.
Enough of that putting it up.

00:07:02.555 --> 00:07:05.658
[ Laughter ]

00:07:05.658 --> 00:07:07.260
-You seem very comfortable.

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:09.328
-I was very comfortable.
You know?

00:07:09.328 --> 00:07:10.696
Yeah, absolutely.

00:07:10.696 --> 00:07:12.398
-You had it, flaunt it.

00:07:12.398 --> 00:07:15.034
Do you feel like Moriarty looked
at you differently after that?

00:07:15.034 --> 00:07:16.469
-Uh, no. But I --

00:07:16.469 --> 00:07:17.603
You know,
he looked at me differently --

00:07:17.603 --> 00:07:19.638
We once had a party,
and I wondered where

00:07:19.638 --> 00:07:21.707
the bowl of hash had gone,
and I found him upside down

00:07:21.707 --> 00:07:25.077
on his back for days like that,
thinking, "Oh, my God!

00:07:25.077 --> 00:07:27.246
The dog is, whatever, stoned
out of his mind in the garden."

00:07:27.246 --> 00:07:28.514
[ Laughter ]

00:07:28.514 --> 00:07:29.815
But he got over it,
like everything else.

00:07:29.815 --> 00:07:32.351
-He got over it, yeah.
I mean, can you blame a dog

00:07:32.351 --> 00:07:35.654
after a photo shoot like this?
He's gonna go...

00:07:35.654 --> 00:07:38.624
-He got extra Alpo.
-He got extra Alpo.

00:07:38.624 --> 00:07:40.726
You've got some other things
coming up, as well.

00:07:40.726 --> 00:07:41.993
"Ballerina," which takes place
in the John Wick universe.

00:07:41.993 --> 00:07:43.229
-"Ballerina" --
that's the --

00:07:43.229 --> 00:07:45.364
that's the
John Wick spin-off with Ana,

00:07:45.364 --> 00:07:47.533
the lovely Ana de Armas.
-Fantastic.

00:07:47.533 --> 00:07:49.334
-Yeah. That's, uh --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:49.334 --> 00:07:51.370
-And, uh -- And then we got
"Kung Fu Panda 4".

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:52.905
-"Kung Fu Panda 4."
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:52.905 --> 00:07:56.409
-You've talked a little bit
about, um, a motivation.

00:07:56.409 --> 00:07:58.443
And somehow, as an actor,
you have motivation,

00:07:58.443 --> 00:08:00.213
and you try to find
a motivation for the role.

00:08:00.213 --> 00:08:02.080
What is your motivation
for Snow Leopard

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:03.649
in "Kung Fu Panda 4"?
[ Laughter ]

00:08:03.649 --> 00:08:05.217
-Uh...

00:08:05.217 --> 00:08:07.486
-So, uh,
he's looking for the check

00:08:07.486 --> 00:08:10.823
constantly across his lonely
trek across the snow.

00:08:10.823 --> 00:08:14.627
[ Laughter ]
"Where's the check?!"

00:08:14.627 --> 00:08:17.262
As it always is.
Just a different location.

00:08:17.262 --> 00:08:18.964
-Well, that's a great message
for the kids.

00:08:18.964 --> 00:08:20.933
You guys...
[ Laughter ]

00:08:20.933 --> 00:08:23.368
Ian McShane, everybody!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:23.368 --> 00:08:25.237
No one else like him!

00:08:25.237 --> 00:08:27.606
"American Star" is in theaters
January 26th.

00:08:27.606 --> 00:08:29.375 line:0%
We'll be right back
with music from Black Pumas.

